1 OCCU VII. principe di Carignano, King of Sardinia 1831-1849, książe Sabaucki, 7th Principe di Carignano (1800-31), succeeded as King of Sardinia and Duca di Savoia (27.4.1831-23.3.1849), King of Sardinia
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Links:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT *[http://www.thepeerage.com/p11445.htm The Peerage]
2 CONT *[http://www.geneall.net/I/per_page.php?id=5553 Geneall]
2 CONT *'''King of Sardinia''' Reign 27 April 1831 – 23 March 1849 '''Predecessor'''  [http://www.geni.com/people/index/6000000008584693992 Charles Felix] '''Successor'''  [http://www.geni.com/people/index/1354718 Victor Emmanuel II]
2 CONT >'''Wikipedia''' [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Albert_of_Sardinia English ][http://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carlo_Alberto_di_Savoia_  Italiano ][http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karl_Albert_%28Sardinien-Piemont%29_ Deutsch], 
2 CONT * Charles Albert (Carlo Alberto Amedeo; 2 October 1798 – 28 July 1849) was the King of Piedmont-Sardinia from 1831 to 1849. He succeeded his distant cousin Charles Felix, and his name is bound with the first Italian statute and th
2 CONC e First War of Independence (1848–49). He abdicated after his forces were defeated by the Austrian army at the Battle of Novara (1849), and died in exile soon thereafter.
2 CONT ===Biography===
2 CONT * He was born in Turin in 1798, to Charles Emmanuel, Prince of Carignano and Maria Cristina of Saxony. His father was a fifth-generation descendant of Thomas Francis, Prince of Carignano, founder of the Savoy-Carignano line of the
2 CONC  House of Savoy. Because none of the sons of Victor Amadeus III themselves had sons, Charles Albert was throughout his life known to be their likely successor on the throne of Sardinia.
2 CONT * He was educated in the intellectually liberal and Francophile atmosphere of Geneva, then in Paris during the First French Empire. Napoleon I of France named him lieutenant of the dragoons in 1814. After the fall of Napoleon, Cha
2 CONC rles Albert returned to Turin. Two mentors were entrusted with countering the dangerous ideas about national liberation Charles had learned in France. However, he continued to display some sympathies with liberals.
2 CONT * In 1821, as regent for the kingdom in the absence of the new king, Charles Felix (then in Modena), he conceded a constitution that was disavowed by the king, who sent him to join the French army in Spain to suppress the liberal 
2 CONC revolution there and restore Ferdinand VII. He distinguished himself at the Battle of Trocadero in 1823, which annihilated hopes of a constitutional monarchy for Spain and also gained him the favour of Austria.
2 CONT * Charles Albert succeeded Charles Felix to the throne of Sardinia in 1831. Although an Italian patriot allegedly opposed to the Austrian hegemony in Northern Italy, he put down the Mazzini conspiracy. He introduced a series of re
2 CONC forms that abolished domestic customs barriers within the kingdom, promulgated a constitutional law code (Statuto Albertino) inspired to those of France and Belgium, and supported the arts and sciences.
2 CONT * During the Revolutions of 1848 he agreed to a constitutional regime that remained in place for the century that the Kingdom of Italy lasted. The same year he declared war on Austria, with the small army supported by volunteers f
2 CONC rom the whole of Italy. However, after his initial victories lost him the support of the Pope and the other Italian monarchs, he was defeated at Battle of Custoza (24 July 1848), being forced to sign an armistice at Vigevano on 9 
2 CONC August. When, pushed by the increasing influence of the Republicans in Piedmont, he attempted to resume the war the next year, the Piedmontese were again crushed by Radetzky's troops at Novara. Rather than redrawing the Statute, h
2 CONC e abdicated in favour of his son, Victor Emmanuel fleeing in exile to Portugal.
2 CONT * He died at Porto the same year. His remains were transferred to the Basilica of Superga.
2 CONT ===Friedrich Engels said of Charles Albert:===
2 CONT * Among the indigenous princes, the number one enemy of Italian freedom was and is Charles Albert. The Italians should bear in mind and repeat every hour the old saying: "God watch over my friends, so that I can watch over my enem
2 CONC ies." From Ferdinand of the House of Bourbon, there is nothing to fear; he has for a long time been discredited. Charles Albert on the other hand calls himself pompously the "liberator of Italy" while on the very people he is supp
2 CONC osed to be liberating he imposes as a condition the yoke of his rule (Neue Rheinische Zeitung No. 73, 12 August 1848).
2 CONT ===Family and children===
2 CONT * In 1817, Charles Albert married his second cousin once removed, Maria Theresa of Austria, the youngest daughter of Ferdinand III, Grand Duke of Tuscany, and Princess Luisa of Naples and Sicily. The couple had the following child
2 CONC ren:
2 CONT * Victor Emmanuel II (1820–78) married Adelaide of Austria
2 CONT * Prince Ferdinand of Savoy (1822–55), Duke of Genoa married Princess Elisabeth of Saxony
2 CONT * Princess Maria Cristina of Savoy (1826–27) died in infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Kaarle Albert (italiaksi Carlo Alberto Amedeo di Savoia; 1798–1849) oli Sardinian kuningas 1831–1849.[1] Hän syntyi Torinossa ja oli etäistä sukua kuninkaalle, mutta koska kuninkaalla ei ollut poikaa, Kaarle Albert tiesi jo nuoren
2 CONC a olevansa todennäköisesti joskus vielä Sardinian kuningas. Hän opiskeli Sveitsissä ja Ranskassa, ja tutustui liberaalien ajatuksiin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vuoden 1848 vallankumousten jälkimainingeissa hän hyväksyi perustuslaillisen monarkian, jonka hallitusmuoto pysyi voimassa sadan vuoden ajan Italian kuningaskunnassa, jonka ensimmäinen kuningas oli hänen poikansa Viktor Emanuel II
2 CONC . Kaarle taisteli Itävaltaa vastaan mutta kärsi tappion Novaran taistelussa ja luopui kruunusta poikansa hyväksi. Hän lähti maanpakoon Portugaliin, missä kuoli myöhemmin samana vuonna.
2 CONT
